---
import SwissLayout from '../layouts/SwissLayout.astro';
import SwissHeader from '../components/SwissHeader.astro';
import SwissFooter from '../components/SwissFooter.astro';

const updated = '26 September 2026';
---

<SwissLayout
  title="Laurent Nyssen — Accessibility"
  description="What this site does to stay usable by everyone, what it does not do yet, and how to tell me when it fails you."
>
  <SwissHeader />

  <main id="contenu" tabindex="-1">
    <section class="swiss-shell pt-[var(--section-space)]">
      <h1 class="swiss-display">Accessibility<span class="text-[var(--accent-texte)]">.</span></h1>
    </section>

    <section class="swiss-shell">
      <div class="swiss-grid gap-y-4 border-t border-[var(--rule)]">
        <h2 class="section-label swiss-meta col-span-6 text-[var(--ink-soft)] md:col-span-2">
          In short
        </h2>

        <p class="swiss-lede col-span-6 md:col-span-8">
          This site aims at WCAG 2.2 level AA. Most of it holds. Two things
          don't, and they are named below rather than left for you to find.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="swiss-shell">
      <div class="swiss-grid gap-y-4 border-t border-[var(--rule)]">
        <h2 class="section-label swiss-meta col-span-6 text-[var(--ink-soft)] md:col-span-2">
          What is in place
        </h2>

        <div class="privacy-body col-span-6 text-[17px] leading-[1.55] md:col-span-8">
          <p>
            Every page is built from headings, lists and landmarks, in the order
            it reads. A skip link comes first. The whole site works from the
            keyboard, and what holds focus says so with a two-pixel ring.
          </p>
          <p>
            Text meets 4.5:1 against its background, in both themes and in all
            six tints. The tint you pick colours the surfaces exactly as it is
            drawn in the palette; where it becomes text, it darkens or lightens
            until it is readable.
          </p>
          <p>
            Projects open and close on click and on Enter, through the browser's
            own disclosure element. Nothing here is a div pretending to be a
            button.
          </p>
          <p>
            If your system asks for reduced motion, it is honoured throughout:
            the showcase stops, the reveals are cut, the cursor goes back to
            being the one your system draws, and the falling shapes on Le
            Polographe never fall.
          </p>
          <p>
            Nothing autoplays with sound, nothing steals focus, nothing runs on
            a timer you have to beat.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="swiss-shell">
      <div class="swiss-grid gap-y-4 border-t border-[var(--rule)]">
        <h2 class="section-label swiss-meta col-span-6 text-[var(--ink-soft)] md:col-span-2">
          What does not hold yet
        </h2>

        <div class="privacy-body col-span-6 text-[17px] leading-[1.55] md:col-span-8">
          <p>
            <strong>The showcase band moves on its own.</strong> It slows under
            the pointer, stops entirely on keyboard focus, and never starts if
            you have asked for reduced motion — but there is no always-visible
            pause button. WCAG asks for one, and it is the next thing I will
            build.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Project images are named, not described.</strong> Their text
            alternative is the project title. It tells you which project you are
            in; it does not tell you what the image shows.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two smaller things: the tool capsule at the bottom of the screen
            turns to the tint on hover, which is faint for the palest tints, and
            the decorative marks — the falling shapes, the mark beside each
            teaser — are drawn in the tint as it is, without correction. None of
            them carry meaning.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="swiss-shell">
      <div class="swiss-grid gap-y-4 border-t border-[var(--rule)]">
        <h2 class="section-label swiss-meta col-span-6 text-[var(--ink-soft)] md:col-span-2">
          How this was checked
        </h2>

        <div class="privacy-body col-span-6 text-[17px] leading-[1.55] md:col-span-8">
          <p>
            By me, on this site, not by an outside auditor: keyboard passes on
            every page, contrast measured on all twelve theme and tint
            combinations, and the pages read with the browser's own tools. No
            formal audit and no user testing with assistive technology has been
            done. That is worth knowing when you read the claim above.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="swiss-shell">
      <div class="swiss-grid gap-y-4 border-t border-[var(--rule)]">
        <h2 class="section-label swiss-meta col-span-6 text-[var(--ink-soft)] md:col-span-2">
          Tell me
        </h2>

        <div class="privacy-body col-span-6 text-[17px] leading-[1.55] md:col-span-8">
          <p>
            If something here blocks you, write to
            <a href="mailto:hello@laurentnyssen.be?subject=Accessibility">hello@laurentnyssen.be</a>.
            Say what you were trying to do and what got in the way. I answer
            within a week, and I would rather hear it than not.
          </p>
          <p class="text-[var(--ink-soft)]">
            This site is a personal portfolio, so the European accessibility
            rules for public bodies do not apply to it. This statement is
            voluntary. Last reviewed {updated}.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <SwissFooter />
</SwissLayout>
